Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  02 REMOVED_MONTH 01 ACCESSIONED ON  02 REMOVED_MONTH 01 at 14 50 CLINICAL DATA  The patient has a left breast mass  Core biopsy shows malignant tumor  type unknown  Clinically  it behaves like carcinoma                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  MALIGNANT SPINDLE CELL TUMOR WITH DENSE INFLAMMATORY INFILTRATE WITH FEATURES SUGGESTIVE OF FIBROBLASTIC SARCOMA  INFLAMMATORY FIBROSARCOMA    See note   NOTE  The neoplasm is composed of cytologically malignant spindle cells that are arranged haphazardly in a collagenous and myxoid stroma  In areas  the tumor is infiltrated by a dense lymphoid infiltrate  and in regions where the infiltrate is most dense  the tumor cells are polyphedral and fewer in number  Immunostains show that the tumor cells are positive for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   repeated twice   weakly  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  on frozen section only  vimentin and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   nd negative for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Cd2   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Cd5   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Cd8   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Cd68   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   ALK 1   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID a  lysosyme  myeloperoxidase   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   cytokeratin  smooth muscle actin  muscle actin  desmin and S 100 protein  Expression of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  raises the possibility of a T cell lymphoma  However  the morphology  the lack of other lymphoid associated antigens and the ultrastructural features of the neoplastic cells  See EM report  are more suggestive of a fibroblastic sarcoma such as inflammatory fibrosarcoma  The tumor does not have the morphology or immunoprofile of a carcinoma  Diagnosis by  Andrew Eric Rosenberg  M D  Signed On  30 REMOVED_MONTH 01 at 16 28 Consulting Pathologist s   Nancy Lee Harris  M D                                                                                      ADDITIONAL DIAGNOSTIC INFORMATION Addendum     In situ hybridization  ISH  performed on a paraffin embedded section reveals NO STAINING with an oligonucleotide probe for EBV encoded RNA  EBER   Added by  Judith Ann Ferry  M D  Signed On  18 REMOVED_MONTH 01 at 17 16     ELECTRON MICROSCOPY Addendum     BREAST BIOPSY  MALIGNANT NEOPLASM WITH FEATURES OF FIBROBLASTIC DIFFERENTIATION  SEE NOTE  NOTE  Unfortunately  the fresh tissue that was submitted for electron microscopy did not contain definitive tumor  Tissue had therefore to be retrieved from paraffin which markedly diminishes the quality of the cells  The cells do  however  seem to demonstrate features of fibroblastic differentiation  They are spindle shaped and contain dilated rough endoplasmic reticulum  Other features are difficult to interpret due to the poor quality of the tissue  Added by  Gunnlaugur P  Nielsen  MD Signed On  07 REMOVED_MONTH 01 at 11 08 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  breast  left   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 0cm portion of firm  yellow  fatty soft tissue  The specimen is inked black and sectioned to reveal a 1 9cm firm well circumscribed mass surrounded by a thin rim of soft  yellow  fibrofatty tissue  The mass has a white tan  smooth cut surface and is of uniform consistency with no evidence of necrosis  One representative section of the mass is frozen as FX 1  and is kept frozen for Warren 5  A small portion of the tumor is saved for Electron Microscopy  Two sections of the tumor are fixed in B   and submitted as cassette A  The remainder of the tissue is fixed in formalin and submitted entirely as cassette B  Resident  JOHN BRANDA  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS  1  BREAST  LEFT   BIOPSY  MALIGNANT TUMOR  AWAITING PERMANENTS FOR CLASSIFICATION  O R  Resident  ARASH RADFAR  MD  PhD O R  Diagnosis by  CHIN LEE WU  MD  PhD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REPORTS TO  Michael Ned Margolies  M D  PART   BREAST LEFT  EXCISIONAL BIOPSY , No.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  14 REMOVED_MONTH 94 ACCESSIONED ON  14 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 13 08 CLINICAL DATA  Abnormal mammogram left breast  Two lesions are noted in A and B                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  A   INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  60   AND INTRADUCTAL CARCINOMA  40    GRADE REMOVED_DATE EXTENDING TO THE INKED RESECTION MARGINS  SEE NOTE  NOTE  Several microcalcifications are seen in slide A and E  No lymphatic or vascular invasion identifed  This tumor measures 1 1cm in diameter  BREAST  LEFT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  B   INFILTRATING  80   AND INTRADUCTAL CARCINOMA  20    GRADE REMOVED_DATE EXTENDING TO THE INKED RESECTION MARGIN  SEE NOTE  NOTE  Microcalcifications are seen in slide M  No lymphatic or vascular invascular invasion  The tumor measures 0 8cm in diameter  Diagnosis by  Austin L  Vickery  M D  Signed On  16 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 13 44                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT  BOTH SPECIMENS   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  07 REMOVED_MONTH 94 at 14 44 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab  labeled  A  Left breast  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm fibronodular fibrous tissue with localizing wire  The specimen is inked and sectioned to reveal a stellate firm white lesion with central hemorrhagic focus measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 8cm extending to the inked margin  Specimen is submitted in cassettes A through F  nodule in A   C  representative sections in cassettes D  E  and F  Representative section of nodule frozen and saved  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab  labeled  B  Left breast  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm fatty fibrous tissue with localizing wire  Inked and sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 4cm firm  white nodule   1  approximately 1cm from inked margin  and a separate irregular white lesion   2  measuring 1 x REMOVED_DATE x 0 5cm at the margin  Nodule in cassette G  second irregular lesion  2 in cassettes H and I  Representative sections in J through N  Representative section of rounded nodule  1 saved frozen  Resident  Simon Bergman  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     GROSS DIAGNOSIS  LEFT BREAST SPECIMEN A   SMALL CARCINOMA  GROSS DIAGNOSIS  LEFT BREAST SPECIMEN B   SMALL CARCINOMA  O R  Resident  Karen C  Deering  MD O R  Diagnosis by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SURGICAL DAY CARE   ACC 3 REPORTS TO  Herbert Charles Hoover Jr   M D  PART   LEFT BREAST LESION SPECIMEN A  B   report_end  , No.
